\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{\b Noted Tuesday, February 10, 2015\b0 , in Winnipeg, and posted on the Yahoo! Groups WordStar group:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{WordStar recognizes an undocumented ".wi" dot command:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{It's a regular dot command, showing a "." in the flag column, not a "1," which indicates a dot command that works best at the top of a page, or a ":" for a merge-print dot command.  (And invalid dot commands show a "?" -- try ".wx" for instance; ".wi" is not invalid.)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{My hope was that it was an undocumented "widow-orphan control" dot command, but it doesn't seem to do anything.  I tried it on its own and with arguments of:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{ .wi 0}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{ .wi 1}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{ .wi 2}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{ .wi off}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{ .wi on}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{ .wi dis }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{It's recognized in WordStar for DOS 4.0 right through to WordStar 7.0.  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{It's not for character-width; that's the ".cw" dot command.  And I doubt it stands for "WordStar International," since, as I said, the command is recognized in WordStar 4.0, and the company was known as MicroPro International back then.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{Anyone know what it's for?  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{Rob}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{\page \b Noted Tuesday, November 9, 2021:\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0{In all of the WordStar program files, there is only one reference to "widow," and that's in WSHELP.OVR, but I can find no way to access that message from within a WordStar document.  There are zero referencs to "orphan."}\par
}
